  "dom_id": "step-q81245",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Calculate ${\u222c}_{R}(4{y}^{3}-2x)\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[2,5]\\times [0,3].$",
  "worked_solution": "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}(4{y}^{3}-2x)\\text{d}A & ={\u222c}_{R}4{y}^{3}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A-{\u222c}_{R}2x\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}4{y}^{3}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A & =(5-2){\\int }_{0}^{3}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3[{y}^{4}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3({3}^{4}-{0}^{4}) \\\\  & =243.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}2x\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A & =(3-0){\\int }_{2}^{5}2x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3[{x}^{2}]_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =3({5}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =63.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}(4{y}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A=243-63=180.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
